A||||4||johnt671||Different headliner ?||||5||
Z||000000||johnt671||12-12-2001||05:48 PM||||I've read through a lot of info here about headliners and only one thing is stopping me from doing it myself.  I don't have a sunroof so my headliner panel isn't flat.  I have a recessed area over  the driver and passenger seats.  How can I get the headliner to smoothly cover this type of panel.  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||12-12-2001||06:11 PM||||Use foam backed headliner material. It's really stretchy||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||12-12-2001||07:24 PM||||Have a friend help keep the material under control.  It helps to have an extra hand or two. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever3000||12-12-2001||07:38 PM||||I learned by screwing up, then taking the board to a shop.  Wasn't bad, though, $25 and it looks great.  They told me <B>don't</B> put the adhesive all over and then expect to get put the material into place.  Rather, set the material on the board, fold about half of it back, apply some adhesive for only <B>one</B> of the indented places.  When it's time, then contact the material to the board and then do some more.  The trick, apparently, is doing a small area at a time.  Don't try to get it all done at once.  Take some time.  Don't forget to mark spots for the visor & hanger screws, unless you're more talented than me at finding the right spot later.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||12-12-2001||08:06 PM||||The headline material does conform nicely to the recessed areas of the headliner board.  However, don't "stretch" the material into place if you can help it.  <P>When I did mine, I applied 3M "heavy duty" spray adhesive (made specifically for headliners) to both the headliner board and the back of the material.  After it dried to the touch, I applied the material by starting in the center of the headliner board (between the two recessed areas), being very careful not to allow either side to make contact, then worked one side at a time. It was very helpful (probably necessary) to have a friend hold the opposite side up to prevent accidental contact.  I slowly "rolled" the material into the recessed area, making contact as I worked from the center of the board to the end.  Repeat for the other side.  The "give and take" of the material allows the material to conform nicely to the corners of the recessed area.  Just remember, the material adheres on contact, so work slowly and carefully. ||||reg||1
